import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cleanthes: an Android password manager, built in the open — FavourDevLabs"
  description="A security case study: how Cleanthes protects keys, sessions and data on Android, what it sends over the network, and what it does not protect."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="section-label">Case study</p>
    <h1>Cleanthes</h1>
    <p class="treatises-lede">
      An Android password manager I build as a security case study. The source is public, so every
      claim below points at code you can read, and I state what it does not protect.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>One random key encrypts the vault, and your password only wraps it</h2>
    <p>
      When you create a vault, Cleanthes generates a random 256-bit key. Entry fields are encrypted
      with it using AES-GCM, with a fresh random 12-byte nonce each time. Your master password never
      encrypts data directly. Password unlock and biometric unlock are two independent ways to unwrap
      the same vault key.
    </p>

    <h2>The master password is stretched 310,000 times first</h2>
    <p>
      PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with 310,000 iterations and a 256-bit output, using random 16-byte salts. The
      hash that verifies a login and the key that wraps the vault key use separate salts.
    </p>

    <h2>Biometric unlock is bound to a hardware-backed key, not just a prompt</h2>
    <p>
      The biometric path wraps the vault key under an Android Keystore AES-256 key that requires user
      authentication. The prompt accepts strong biometrics only, and the vault key cannot be unwrapped
      unless the prompt returns the unlocked cipher. Hardware backing depends on the device, and
      Cleanthes does not require StrongBox.
    </p>
    <p>
      Autofill is weaker by design. It asks for a biometric confirmation on top of an already unlocked
      session. That is an identity check, not a cryptographic one.
    </p>

    <h2>The key is in memory only while the vault is unlocked</h2>
    <p>
      A session manager holds it. The app locks after 30 seconds in the background or 2 minutes
      without activity. Code reaches the key only through a scoped function that returns nothing when
      the vault is locked, and the timers have unit tests.
    </p>
    <p>
      Locking drops the reference. It does not overwrite the key bytes, and decrypted values are
      ordinary Kotlin strings. Anything readable from process memory while the vault is unlocked is
      exposed.
    </p>

    <h2>Secret screens resist capture, and backups are off</h2>
    <p>
      Login, setup, the autofill prompt and the ten authenticated screens set the secure-window flag.
      Screenshots and screen recording are blocked, and the content is hidden in the app switcher. App
      backup is disabled. Release builds are minified and resource-shrunk with R8.
    </p>

    <h2>Two features make network requests, and each sends little</h2>
    <p>
      The favicon feature sends only a site's hostname to my own proxy, then caches the icon on the
      device. The proxy therefore learns which sites you store logins for.
    </p>
    <p>
      The breach check runs only when you tap it, after re-authentication. It sends the first five
      characters of the password's SHA-1 hash to the Pwned Passwords range API with padding on, and
      compares the rest on the device.
    </p>

    <h2>A device check that reports, and does not enforce</h2>
    <p>
      Settings shows whether the device's Keystore reports software, TEE or StrongBox security. It
      generates a throwaway key, validates the certificate chain against Google's attestation roots
      bundled in the app, and reads the security level and boot state from the certificate. It does not
      gate unlocking, and it checks a probe key, not the vault key.
    </p>
    <p>
      Known gaps: it does not compare the challenge, it does not read the device-locked flag, and
      revocation checking is off.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does not protect</h2>
    <p>
      The category, timestamps, the favourite flag, and the TOTP issuer, digits, period and algorithm
      are stored unencrypted. The database file as a whole is not encrypted, only individual fields.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      21 Gradle modules: feature modules, a domain layer, and api/impl splits for data, session and
      attestation, with fakes for tests. Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Hilt, Room 2.7 with exported schemas
      and migrations, Java 17.
    </p>
  </div>

